+# Deploying the Tip Jar frontend to IPFS
+
+The site ships as a **static export** (`packages/nextjs/out`) pinned on IPFS. There is no server, no API route
+and no image optimizer at runtime.
+
+> Nothing has been uploaded from this repo. The commands below are the exact steps to run; every `<placeholder>`
+> is yours to fill in.
+>
+> Any `packages/nextjs/out` in your working tree was produced with the **local fork** configuration, as a smoke
+> test of this pipeline. Rebuild after step 1 before uploading anything.
+
+## 1. Point the app at a real chain first
+
+The local setup in [README.md](./README.md) targets the Anvil fork (chain 31337, RPC `http://127.0.0.1:8545`).
+Exporting that configuration would ship a site that asks every visitor's browser to reach their own localhost.
+Before building for production:
+
+1. **Deploy the jar to Base** (needs a funded keystore account — `yarn account:import` or `yarn generate`):
+
+   ```bash
+   yarn deploy --network base
+   yarn verify --network base      # optional, needs ETHERSCAN_API_KEY for BaseScan
+   ```
+
+   This rewrites `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts` with the `8453` entry the frontend needs.
+   `externalContracts.ts` already carries USDC for `8453` (same address as on the fork).
+
+   The deployer becomes the owner, and the owner is the only way tips ever leave the jar. On a real deploy hand
+   that to a multisig: `transferOwnership(<safe>)` then `acceptOwnership()` from the safe (the jar uses
+   `Ownable2Step`, and `renounceOwnership` reverts, so a fat-fingered address cannot strand the balance).
+
+2. **Switch the target network** in `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`:
+
+   ```ts
+   targetNetworks: [chains.base],
+   ```
+
+3. **Set real API keys.** The scaffold defaults are shared and rate-limited. A static export inlines
+   `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` at build time, so put them in `packages/nextjs/.env.local` (or pass them on the build
+   command) *before* building:
+
+   ```bash
+   NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY=<your key>
+   NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID=<your project id>
+   ```
+
+## 2. Build configuration (already in this repo)
+
+Three pieces make the export work — listed here so a failed build is diagnosable, not so you have to add them.
+
+**`packages/nextjs/next.config.ts`** — static export, one directory per route, no image optimizer:
+
+```ts
+if (process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD === "true") {
+  nextConfig.output = "export";
+  nextConfig.trailingSlash = true;
+  nextConfig.images = { unoptimized: true };
+}
+```
+
+**`packages/nextjs/utils/scaffold-eth/getMetadata.ts`** — uses `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` as the metadata
+base. A static export has no request context, so the origin must be baked in at build time or every Open Graph
+URL and image points at `http://localhost:3000`.
+
+**`packages/nextjs/package.json`** — the build scripts export `NODE_OPTIONS="--no-experimental-webstorage"`:
+
+```json
+"build": "yarn lint && NODE_OPTIONS=\"--no-experimental-webstorage\" next build",
+"build:ipfs": "rm -rf .next out && NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true yarn build"
+```
+
+On Node 25 the built-in `localStorage` global exists but has no methods unless a backing file is configured.
+The wallet libraries detect the global and call `getItem()` during prerender, which fails with
+`TypeError: localStorage.getItem is not a function`. Setting it in the script makes it process-level, so Next's
+build workers inherit it; putting the equivalent only in `instrumentation.ts` or `next.config.ts` does not fix
+the workers. `NODE_OPTIONS="--localstorage-file=.node-localstorage"` works too if you prefer real storage.
+
+## 3. Build
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://<your-domain-or-gateway-url>" yarn build:ipfs
+```
+
+From the repo root the same build is `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://<...>" yarn next:build:ipfs`.
+`build:ipfs` removes `.next` and `out` first, so a stale export can never be mistaken for a fresh one.
+
+## 4. Verify `out/` before uploading
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+
+# One directory with an index.html per route (/, /debug, /blockexplorer, ...)
+ls out/*/index.html
+test -f out/index.html && test -f out/debug/index.html && echo "routes ok"
+
+# Open Graph metadata carries the production origin, not localhost
+grep -o 'property="og:url" content="[^"]*"' out/index.html
+grep -o 'property="og:image" content="[^"]*"' out/index.html
+grep -rl "localhost:3000" out --include='*.html'          # expect no output
+
+# The chain and contract the bundle will actually talk to
+grep -rlF "<jar address from step 1>" out/_next/static | head -1   # expect a match
+grep -rl "127.0.0.1:8545" out/_next/static | head -1               # expect no output for a Base build
+
+# Smoke test the export exactly as a gateway serves it
+npx --yes serve out -l 8080 &
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "root %{http_code}\n" http://localhost:8080/
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "debug %{http_code}\n" -L http://localhost:8080/debug/
+```
+
+A build that still matches `127.0.0.1:8545` was made with `targetNetworks: [chains.foundry]` — that is the
+signal you skipped step 1, and every visitor would get a site pointed at their own machine.
+
+Open `http://localhost:8080` and confirm the feed and the form render before you upload anything. Serving from
+the server root like this matches how the site must be served in production — see the next section for why.
+
+## 5. Serve it from a domain root, not a path gateway
+
+The export references its assets absolutely (`<script src="/_next/static/chunks/...">`), which is what
+`next build` with `output: "export"` produces — there is no supported relative-asset mode. So the site only
+works where the CID is the root of the origin:
+
+| Serving style | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Subdomain gateway — `https://<cidv1>.ipfs.dweb.link/` | works |
+| ENS / DNSLink domain — `https://<name>.eth.limo/` | works |
+| Path gateway — `https://gateway/ipfs/<cid>/` | HTML returns 200, every `/_next/...` asset 404s, page renders blank |
+
+The path-gateway failure is quiet: `curl` on the HTML says 200 while the browser shows an empty page. Verify on
+a subdomain gateway or the ENS domain, and read a "200 but blank" report as this rather than as a build
+problem. (If you are pinned to a path gateway, the only fix is a two-pass build with
+`assetPrefix: "/ipfs/<cid>"` — you have to build, upload, then rebuild with the CID you just got. Prefer a
+subdomain gateway.)
+
+## 6. Upload
+
+Pick one. All three produce a CID for the `out` directory — **record it**.
+
+```bash
+# A. bgipfs (the scaffold's built-in path: builds and uploads in one step)
+yarn ipfs
+
+# B. a local Kubo node
+ipfs add -r --cid-version 1 -Q packages/nextjs/out
+ipfs pin remote add --service=<service> --name tipjar <cid>     # so it survives your node going offline
+
+# C. Storacha / web3.storage
+npx --yes @web3-storage/w3cli up packages/nextjs/out
+```
+
+## 7. Verify after uploading
+
+```bash
+CID=<cid from step 6>                      # base32 CIDv1: ipfs cid base32 <cid>
+SITE="https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link"         # subdomain gateway, so absolute asset paths resolve
+
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "root  %{http_code}\n" -L "$SITE/"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "debug %{http_code}\n" -L "$SITE/debug/"      # expect 200, not 404
+curl -s -L "$SITE/" | grep -o 'property="og:\(url\|image\)" content="[^"]*"'
+
+# The JS actually loads — this is the check a path gateway silently fails
+ASSET=$(curl -s -L "$SITE/" | grep -o 'src="/_next/static/chunks/[^"]*"' | head -1 | sed 's/src="//;s/"//')
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "asset %{http_code}  ($ASSET)\n" -L "$SITE$ASSET"
+```
+
+- A root that loads does **not** prove the exported routes resolve — check at least one non-home route, with
+  the trailing slash, and at least one `_next` asset.
+- If the CID is unchanged after a rebuild, the uploaded bytes are identical. Check what you built and what you
+  uploaded before blaming gateway caching.
+- Finally, load the gateway URL in a browser, connect a wallet on Base, and send one small real tip. The feed
+  entry and the "In the jar" stat are the end-to-end proof.
+
+## 8. ENS content hash (only after the CID is reviewed)
+
+```bash
+ipfs cid base32 $CID                       # gateways and ENS want the base32 v1 form
+# set contenthash to ipfs://<cid> via https://app.ens.domains, or directly:
+cast send <resolver> "setContenthash(bytes32,bytes)" $(cast namehash <name>.eth) <encoded contenthash> \
+  --rpc-url mainnet --account <your keystore>
+
+# confirm the resolver returns the CID you approved, and that the public gateway serves it
+cast call <resolver> "contenthash(bytes32)(bytes)" $(cast namehash <name>.eth) --rpc-url mainnet
+curl -sI "https://<name>.eth.limo" | head -1
+```
+
+Update the content hash only after the CID above has been loaded and reviewed — the ENS record is the part
+users actually see.
